ies act but we  won't use it uh for any longer that is  necessary  i'll take your point david  um i think clearly the the police have  have used and and the measures that we  brought forward in the emergency act um  appropriately and to to significant  effects we're pleased with the progress  that's being made we're monitoring on a  daily and now even hourly basis the  threat environment with our senior  officials and with law enforcement and  we are committed  to to ending this emergency at the  earliest opportunity but clearly we're  not out of the emergency yet and and so  we'll continue to monitor that very  carefully um i hope that satisfactory  david  we bonjour has  what is the threshold to remove it  because the truckers could come back in  two months three months so does that  mean we have to keep it uh for another  two three months  this is something that we're thinking  about of course  at some point  once it's lifted  we'll have to remain vigilant  as to what could happen afterwards but  we still feel  that  now  right  keeping an eye on this each day and see  when we can  but every day we're going to reevaluate  and we hope that we won't keep it for  one day longer than necessary hi there  it's andy bergeron for ctv national news  my question is from mr freeland i  believe uh two conservative mps say that  their constituents who donated small  amounts about 50 or so have had their  bank accounts frozen i'm wondering if  this is a case of a mistaken use of  powers granted to banks under the  emergencies act or is the government  going after  any donor including small donors  uh i'll turn to minister freeland in in  a second but  the measures that we put in place are  designed and focused on ensuring uh that  people in the current illegal  occupations leave that has been the  intent and the focus right now if there  are specific cases that conservative  ministers can bring forward to highlight  uh where that is uh is not the case we  would happily look at